COURSES += [
c("SWP-039","Professional Recording Consent and Confidentiality","professional-recording-consent-and-confidentiality","Professional Recording\nConsent and Confidentiality",
  "Prepare relevant, accurate professional records and identify when consent, disclosure or data-protection questions need authorised review.",[
m("Purposeful and accountable records","Write a concise record that distinguishes information sources, interpretation and an agreed action.",[
"A professional record supports a defined purpose: understanding the concern, explaining a decision, coordinating an action or reviewing support. Before collecting a detail, ask why it is relevant to that purpose and who needs access. More information does not automatically create a better record. A note that mixes unrelated personal history with a current referral can obscure the decision and increase unnecessary exposure. Use the service's approved format while keeping the person's concern and the reason for the action clear. [S2, S1]",
"Distinguish the person's report, another source's report, your observation and your interpretation. Record uncertainty rather than presenting a hypothesis as a finding. Neutral language can describe a difficult interaction without making a character judgement. For example, record that a person declined a proposed appointment and the reason they gave, rather than labelling them uncooperative. Where wording could materially affect a decision, check your understanding with the person when appropriate and explain any difference that remains. [S1, R6]",
"A useful progress note identifies the date and purpose of contact, relevant information, the person's expressed preference, action taken, rationale, outstanding tasks and review arrangements. These are teaching prompts rather than a universal statutory template. The service context and applicable requirements determine what the formal record must contain. Document through the authorised system and distinguish the date of an event from the date a later note is written. Do not make a late entry appear contemporaneous or sign as though you authored someone else's account. [S2]",
"The 2026 documentation scoping review by Knechtel and colleagues maps a substantial literature but also identifies uneven coverage across social work domains. It reminds learners to examine the purpose and setting of documentation rather than assume one practice fits every service. The review does not establish that this course's prompts improve outcomes or meet every legal requirement. Use the prompts to support transparent reasoning, then check the required local form, access arrangements and supervisory expectations. [R9]"],
"Fictional case: Sade reports missing a referral appointment because the time changed. A useful note identifies that this is her account, records the change that needs checking and names the worker who will contact the service. It does not present the account as verified misconduct.",
"Write a 150-word progress note for Sade. Label information sources, distinguish a hypothesis from a fact and include an action owner and review date.",
"A strong note is relevant, neutral and reviewable. It identifies the source of each material claim and does not use a broad label where a specific description is available.",["S2","S1","R6","R9"],[
("A record should serve an identified purpose.","A referral needs information about access barriers. Which recording approach is strongest?","Include relevant barriers and the reason they matter to the referral.",["Copy the entire history because greater volume always improves a note.","Collect unrelated sensitive details in case they might be useful someday.","Omit the purpose because the form already has a title."],"Purpose helps determine relevance and supports an understandable decision record."),
("Reports and verified facts remain distinguishable.","Sade reports a changed appointment time. How should the note describe it initially?","Record Sade's account and the verification task separately.",["Record that the service definitely changed the appointment without checking.","Exclude Sade's account because it is not independently confirmed.","Replace the reported event with a judgement about her reliability."],"The person's report is relevant while its verification status stays visible."),
("Neutral wording describes behaviour and context.","Which entry best supports a defensible record?","The person declined the appointment and reported a conflict with caring duties.",["The person was difficult and had a bad attitude.","The person will never engage with services.","The person's choice proves they do not need support."],"Specific description avoids unsupported conclusions about character or future behaviour."),
("Late entries should preserve event and entry timing.","A worker writes a note the day after a contact. What should be clear?","The date of contact and the date the note was entered.",["Only the earlier date so the record appears contemporaneous.","A colleague's signature without identifying the actual author.","No date because timing might cause questions."],"Transparent timing preserves the history of the record."),
("Documentation research does not validate a universal template.","What follows from the documentation scoping review?","Consider the service domain and evidence limits when using a recording approach.",["One template is proven appropriate for every social work setting.","A large literature removes the need for local requirements.","Research-gap findings establish legal compliance."],"The review maps varied scholarship rather than certifying a universal recording form."),
]),
m("Consent purpose and lawful information use","Identify the purpose and authority for processing or sharing information and recognise questions requiring data-protection advice.",[
"Consent is a meaningful process when a person understands a specific choice and can express a preference without a misleading promise. Explain what information is proposed for use or sharing, the purpose, the intended recipient and relevant limits. Check understanding in an accessible way. A signature can document an agreement but does not by itself establish that the person understood it or that every future use is permitted. Record the scope and any relevant condition of the agreement through the approved process. [S1, S5]",
"In Nigeria, the NDP Act and the NDPC's 2025 General Application and Implementation Directive provide the relevant data-protection framework. The GAID addresses processing principles, lawful bases and consent accountability. A worker should establish the service's authorised purpose and basis for processing before relying on a form or sharing arrangement. Consent is not a universal substitute for that assessment. Additional requirements and exceptions may apply to sensitive data, children and other circumstances. Seek the organisation's data-protection or legal advice rather than inventing a basis for a difficult case. [S5]",
"Distinguish permission to participate in a service from a specific information-sharing decision. A person may agree to an appointment while wanting clarification about a proposed disclosure. Discuss the question, identify what the service genuinely requires and avoid promising that information can always remain secret. If a duty or justified exception may limit confidentiality, check the applicable authority and explain relevant limits where appropriate and safe. Another professional's interest in a case does not, by itself, establish entitlement to the complete file. [S1, S2]",
"When consent is the basis used, follow the approved process for recording it, reviewing its scope and handling withdrawal. A new purpose or recipient should prompt a fresh check rather than an assumption that an old agreement covers everything. Consider what minimum relevant information can meet the justified purpose, who is authorised to send it and which secure route applies. If the basis or conditions remain uncertain, seek timely authorised advice and record the decision process. Urgent safeguarding or emergency duties still require the relevant local response procedure. [S5, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a service asks for Sade's full record to consider transport support. The worker clarifies the purpose, checks the service's authorised basis and the applicable information-sharing conditions, and discusses relevant information with Sade. A previous appointment consent form is not treated as unlimited permission.",
"Prepare an information-use decision note. Identify purpose, proposed recipient, relevant information, authority that must be checked, the person's understanding and the advice needed before acting.",
"A good note distinguishes consent from other authority questions and records uncertainty. It does not state that every processing activity has the same lawful basis or that a signature permits unrestricted disclosure.",["S1","S5","S2"],[
("A signature does not establish unlimited informed permission.","A signed form is presented as authority for a new disclosure. What should be checked?","Its scope, the person's understanding and the proposed purpose and recipient.",["Only whether a signature appears anywhere in the file.","Whether the recipient finds the information interesting.","Whether copying the full file would be administratively convenient."],"The actual information-use decision needs a defined scope and appropriate authority."),
("The lawful basis requires an authorised assessment.","A learner is unsure which basis permits processing in a Nigerian service. What should they do?","Use the organisation's data-protection or legal advice to check the applicable basis and conditions.",["Choose consent automatically for every activity without further assessment.","Copy a basis from an overseas example without checking local requirements.","Assume the form's existence resolves the question."],"The applicable framework and context must inform the decision; a learner should not invent legal authority."),
("Service participation and disclosure are distinct choices.","A person agrees to an appointment but asks about a referral disclosure. What is appropriate?","Discuss the specific disclosure, its purpose and relevant conditions separately.",["Treat attendance as agreement to every future disclosure.","Assume a question about disclosure means the person rejects all support.","Avoid explaining the recipient because the service is helpful."],"Agreement to a service does not remove the need to address a particular information use."),
("A new purpose prompts a renewed scope check.","A record collected for support planning is requested for a different purpose. What should happen?","Check the proposed purpose, basis and conditions before reusing it.",["Rely on the original collection decision without reviewing the change.","Send everything because the organisation already holds it.","Ask the recipient to determine all authority after receipt."],"Purpose and authority should be checked before the new use, not assumed from possession."),
("Disclosure should be relevant and securely authorised.","Once a justified referral purpose is established, what should the worker consider?","The minimum relevant information, authorised sender and secure route.",["The largest possible file to avoid later requests.","A personal account because it is easier to access.","All historical details regardless of their relevance."],"A justified purpose still requires proportionate handling and secure delivery."),
]),
m("Secure access corrections and digital tools","Use approved access and correction processes while maintaining confidentiality and an accurate record history.",[
"Confidentiality depends on everyday handling as well as decisions about disclosure. Use approved accounts, devices, storage and access arrangements. Confirm the intended recipient before sending information and avoid exposing a person's details in a public setting. An access permission should match the task, rather than assume that everyone in an organisation needs every file. Follow local requirements for retention and disposal; this course does not prescribe a universal retention period for all records or services. [S5, S2]",
"People may ask to see or correct information about them. Route the request through the authorised process, establish identity appropriately and obtain guidance where third-party information or other restrictions are involved. Do not assume that a request gives unrestricted access to another person's information. Where an error is identified, use a transparent amendment process that preserves the relevant history. Distinguish correcting an inaccurate fact from recording a person's disagreement with a professional interpretation, and explain the next review route. [S5, S1]",
"Digital assistance can make drafting easier while creating new accuracy and confidentiality risks. A worker remains responsible for checking a note, whether it began as typed text, speech recognition or an AI suggestion. Do not put identifiable records into an unapproved tool or accept generated details as observed facts. Check that the authorised system and data-handling conditions fit the task. If information is sent to the wrong recipient or another incident occurs, follow the service's incident process promptly and seek appropriate advice rather than attempting a private cover-up. [S5, S2]",
"Review the record as a communication that may affect the person and future decisions. Check relevance, source attribution, dates, meaning and the agreed next action. The communication review supports inviting clarification, while the documentation review highlights the diversity of recording contexts. Neither paper proves that a particular digital tool or note template is safe or effective. Combine professional judgement, the person's account, approved systems and supervision. A concise, accurate record should make it easier to see both the decision and the limits of the evidence used. [R6, R9]"],
"Fictional case: an automated draft adds a detail that Sade never reported. The worker checks the draft against the contact record and removes the unsupported statement before saving. If an inaccurate entry has already been stored, the worker follows the authorised amendment process.",
"Review a fictional note containing an incorrect date, an unsupported interpretation and an unnecessary personal detail. State how each issue should be handled and what needs an authorised correction or incident process.",
"A strong review preserves an honest history, removes unsupported claims through the right process and limits information to its purpose. It does not silently rewrite an existing record or assume a tool takes responsibility for accuracy.",["S5","S2","S1","R6","R9"],[
("Access should match the authorised task.","A colleague requests a file unrelated to their role. What should the worker check?","The task, authority and appropriate access route before disclosure.",["Whether the colleague works anywhere in the same organisation.","Whether sharing the full file would be quicker.","Whether the file has already been digitised."],"Organisational membership alone does not establish a need for every record."),
("Retention periods depend on the applicable requirements.","Which retention statement is appropriate for a learner?","Check the service's authorised schedule and applicable requirements.",["Use one fixed period for every service and type of record.","Keep all personal data indefinitely without a purpose review.","Delete any record immediately whenever space is limited."],"Retention must follow the relevant framework and service obligations."),
("Corrections preserve a transparent record history.","An existing note contains an incorrect date. What is the defensible response?","Use the authorised amendment process and preserve the relevant history.",["Silently replace the original without an amendment trail.","Change the date to make the service performance look better.","Ignore the error because an earlier note cannot be questioned."],"Accuracy and transparency should be addressed together."),
("Generated text requires human verification and approved data handling.","An AI draft adds an event that was not reported. What must the worker do?","Check the source information and correct the unsupported content through the appropriate process.",["Keep the event because fluent text is likely accurate.","Treat the tool as responsible for the professional record.","Send identifiable files to another unapproved tool for confirmation."],"The worker retains responsibility for accuracy and confidentiality."),
("Information incidents need the authorised response process.","A worker discovers that information went to the wrong recipient. What should happen?","Follow the incident process promptly and obtain appropriate advice.",["Hide the event if no complaint has arrived.","Assume recalling an email removes every responsibility.","Wait for the next routine record review regardless of the incident."],"A prompt authorised response allows the service to assess and address the incident."),
]),
]),
]

